{"step_index":224,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-16T00:52:08Z","content":"That is completely normal! \n\nWindows associates `.xml` files with the **Microsoft Edge** browser by default (and hides the `.xml` extension), which is why it displays the Edge icon and calls it a \"Microsoft Edge HTML Document\".\n\n### ⚠️ Important:\n**Do not double-click that file from Windows Explorer** (if you double-click it, Windows will just open it in your web browser showing raw code).\n\n---\n\n### How to open it inside DaVinci Resolve:\n\n1. Launch **DaVinci Resolve** and open your project (or create an \"Untitled Project\").\n2. In Resolve's top menu, go to:\n   **File** $\\rightarrow$ **Import** $\\rightarrow$ **Timeline...**  \n   *(or press **`Ctrl + Shift + I`** on your keyboard)*\n3. In the file window that opens **inside Resolve**, navigate to your **Downloads** folder.\n4. Select **`RLCF_Sunday_No_Singing.xml`** (or the `.edl` file) and click **Open**.\n5. When the **\"Load XML...\"** options window pops up, leave the defaults checked and click **OK**.\n\n> **Note:** If DaVinci Resolve asks: *\"Do you want to select a directory to search for clips?\"*, click **Yes** and choose your **Downloads** folder so it links to `RLCF Sunday Meeting Livestreaming - September 13, 2026.mp4`.\n\nYour timeline will immediately appear on the Edit page with all the singing segments removed and all the speaking sections seamlessly lined up!","thinking":"The file extension might be misleading; the system could be misinterpreting the file type. The \"HTML Document\" designation suggests a possible association with a web browser's handling of the file, despite the EDL file also existing. It's possible the operating system has a default program or handler assigned that prioritizes Edge for certain file types, or that the file contains some HTML-like markup.\n\n\n"}
