{"step_index":270,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-16T01:03:05Z","content":"You caught it! It's grayed out and locked to `01:00:00:00` because the top checkbox is telling Resolve to use the project default.\n\nHere is how to unlock it:\n\n1. **Uncheck** the first box:  \n   `[ ] Automatically set project settings`  \n   *(The `Timeline start timecode` box will immediately light up and become editable!)*\n\n2. Change **`01:00:00:00`** to **`00:00:00:00`**.\n\n3. Keep `[ ] Automatically import source clips into media pool` **unchecked**.\n\n4. Click **Ok**.\n\nWith the timeline starting at `00:00:00:00`, the timecodes will align with the video in your Media Pool!","thinking":"When a new project is created in DaVinci Resolve, the default timeline start timecode is pre-set in the project's general settings. The \"Timeline start timecode\" field is grayed out, indicating it's inherited from the project settings. The displayed value, `01:00:00:00`, reflects this global project default.\n\n\nIf \"Automatically set project settings\" is checked, DaVinci Resolve likely takes the timeline start timecode from the XML or project settings, which might explain the grayed-out field showing `01:00:00:00`. Unchecking this option should enable manual editing of the timecode. It's unclear why it's showing `01:00:00:00` instead of another value; further investigation is required to understand where that default comes from. The user's observation about the persistent timecode suggests that clicking \"Ok\" might not resolve the issue.\n\n\nThe discrepancy between the XML's stated timecode and Resolve's display likely stems from the interplay between project settings and import behavior. The greyed-out field in Resolve indicates it's inheriting a default value, possibly from the project's global settings, overriding the XML's specific `<timecode>` tag. Manual override of project settings becomes possible by unchecking the \"Automatically set project settings\" option, allowing direct editing of the timeline start timecode.\n\n\n"}
