{"step_index":272,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-16T01:03:39Z","content":"In DaVinci Resolve, that field is grayed out because it is locked by your **Project Settings**. \n\nHere is how to set it to `00:00:00:00` directly in Resolve:\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Change Project Setting\n1. Click **Cancel** on that \"Load XML...\" window.\n2. Click the **Gear icon (⚙️)** in the very bottom-right corner of DaVinci Resolve to open **Project Settings**.\n3. In the left menu, click **General Options**.\n4. Near the top, find **Timeline start timecode** (it currently says `01:00:00:00`).\n5. Change it to **`00:00:00:00`**.\n6. Click **Save** in the bottom right.\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Import the Timeline\nNow when you import:\n1. Go to **File** $\\rightarrow$ **Import** $\\rightarrow$ **Timeline...**\n2. Select [`RLCF_Sunday_No_Singing.xml`](file:///C:/Users/djtwo/Downloads/RLCF_Sunday_No_Singing.xml).\n3. The timeline start timecode will now be **`00:00:00:00`**!\n4. Make sure `[ ] Automatically import source clips into media pool` is **unchecked**.\n5. Click **Ok**.\n\n*(You can also select [`RLCF_Sunday_No_Singing.edl`](file:///C:/Users/djtwo/Downloads/RLCF_Sunday_No_Singing.edl) which uses the exact same `00:00:00:00` timecode).*","thinking":"The \"Timeline start timecode\" is grayed out. The application either uses timecode specified within the imported XML file or defaults to the timecode defined in the general project settings. Therefore, to modify this setting, project configuration must be inspected. The relevant settings are located within a specific, dedicated settings area accessible through a project-level configuration interface.\n\n\nThe discrepancy arises from a mismatch between the timeline's start timecode and the source clip's timecode extents. Specifically, the \"timecode extents\" error pertains to the source clip's timecode, not the timeline ruler's. Investigate the Media Pool clip's attributes for its assigned starting timecode. Determine if the default behavior for files without embedded timecode assigns a timecode of `00:00:00:00` or `01:00:00:00`, and if project settings override this.\n\n\nAnalyzing a previous XML import, a \"timecode extents\" error occurred because the imported file lacked timecode information, while the clip item in the timeline had defined in/out points. The linking failed because the settings prevented matching via file extension and also prevented automatic source clip import, relying instead on reel name, clip name, and timecode matching.\n\nConsidering another approach, perhaps an EDL file could resolve the timecode discrepancy. Importing an EDL file might provide a more reliable method.\n\n\nEDL files may offer a simpler import process than XML, bypassing complex DOM hierarchies. The application presents a distinct dialog upon EDL import, offering \"Select Source Clips\" or \"Conform Options\".\n\nA direct approach involves modifying the project setting for the timeline start timecode. The relevant setting resides in the general project configuration options, which has been verified to be accessible through project settings, that is located on a specific settings tab within the configuration.\n\n\n"}
