{"step_index":274,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-16T01:05:03Z","thinking":"Conform options are visible within the General Options of Project Settings. The settings pertain to how timecode is handled (embedded or from frame count), partial clips, automatic conformity of missing clips, and reel name assistance. This indicates an area focused on media synchronization and alignment within the project.\n\n\n\"Use timecode\" settings are under \"Conform Options\", which are within \"General Options\". The goal is to determine the location of \"Timeline start timecode\" within DaVinci Resolve's Project Settings, specifically if it is in \"General Options\" or \"Master Settings\". Earlier versions had it in General Options. The \"Conform Options\" and \"Color\" sections are visible and the \"Use timecode\" setting is observed within \"Conform Options\".\n\n\nIf an MP4 lacks embedded timecode and Resolve's settings are set to use embedded timecode, the software's behavior needs clarification. The clip's presumed start timecode in the Media Pool requires investigation. The user's preferences, particularly the \"Sort timeline using reel name and timecode\" option, influence timeline organization.\n\nThe location of \"Timeline start timecode\" is being explored. Upon timeline creation, this setting is accessible directly. The user preferences also may influence or control this feature.\n\n\nThe \"Timeline start timecode\" default is located within the User Preferences under the Editing settings. The primary issue appears to be an error during XML import due to timecode mismatches, suggesting investigation into how Resolve handles clips without embedded timecode. DaVinci Resolve assigns `00:00:00:00` as the start TC for MP4s lacking timecode, with duration-based end TC. The goal is to analyze the XML timecode values and compare them to the Media Pool to understand the mismatch. The next step is comparing XML and resolve's timecode.\n\n\n`<start>` and `<end>` in the FCPXML represent the clip's timeline position, with `<start>` being zero-indexed relative to the sequence's start.  This contrasts with `<in>` and `<out>` on the `<clipitem>`, which define the source frames within the media file. The `<file>` tag's attributes contain duration and frame rate information. The FCPXML timeline position is critical, especially when clips do not have embedded timecode, as DaVinci Resolve seems to interpret media's missing timecode as starting at `00:00:00:00`.\n\n\nThe agent is trying to determine if the imported MP4's frame rate is correctly recognized as 30.0 fps in DaVinci Resolve. It's comparing data from the MP4's file information with DaVinci Resolve's timeline and project settings. The \"Timeline start timecode\" setting is confirmed to be in the User Preferences, not Project Settings as initially suspected.\n\n\nThe location of \"Timeline start timecode\" is confirmed to be within User Preferences under Editing settings, not Project Settings. The initial focus on \"Conform Options\" was a misdirection. An alternate approach might avoid the complexities of XML errors, possibly by importing an EDL file containing the desired edits or, even better, using a Python script.  DaVinci Resolve has a built-in console for Python scripting, which could automate timeline creation and editing directly.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"Get-Content \"C:\\ProgramData\\Blackmagic Design\\DaVinci Resolve\\Support\\Developer\\Scripting\\README.txt\" -Raw | Select-String -Pattern \"AppendToTimeline\" -Context 3,5","Cwd":"C:\\Users\\djtwo","IsDaemon":false,"WaitMsBeforeAsync":5000,"toolAction":"Check AppendToTimeline in Resolve API docs","toolSummary":"Check Resolve API docs"}}]}
